course: biology
lesson: 16
title: The great apes: chimpanzees, bonobos, gorillas, orangutans
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The great apes are four kinds of big, tailless, very clever animals: chimpanzees and bonobos, gorillas, and orangutans, and every one of them is endangered.
explain: A silverback gorilla beating his chest looks terrifying, but he is mostly a gentle vegetarian munching about 18 kilograms of leaves a day, and the chest beat is a show that helps avoid real fights.
explain: Apes use tools and know themselves: a chimp fishes termites out of a mound with a grass stem, an orangutan weaves a fresh leafy bed every night, and chimps in a mirror touch a mark on their own face.
explain: Orangutans live in Borneo and Sumatra, gorillas, chimps and bonobos in Africa, and their forests are being cut down for farms and oil palm, while apes have babies so slowly that their numbers take a very long time to recover.
fact: Chimpanzees push a grass stem or stick into termite holes and lick off the termites that bite it; Jane Goodall saw this in Gombe, Tanzania, in 1960, when people still thought only humans made tools.
fact: A silverback is an adult male gorilla whose back turns silver from about 12 years old; he beats his chest with cupped hands, not fists, and the pops can be heard about 1 kilometre away.
fact: Gorillas are almost entirely plant eaters, eating leaves, stems, shoots and fruit, and a big male can eat about 18 kilograms of plants a day.
fact: Orangutans build a new nest high in the trees every night by bending and weaving branches, sometimes with a pillow or a roof of leaves.
fact: Orangutan babies stay with their mothers for about 7 or 8 years, longer than any other animal except humans; orang-utan means person of the forest in Malay.
fact: Bonobos look like slim chimpanzees, live only south of the wide Congo River, which apes cannot swim across, while chimps live to the north of it, have groups led by females, and often settle quarrels peacefully and share food.
fact: Chimpanzees eat mostly fruit but sometimes hunt monkeys like the colobus as a team, each taking a role, and share the meat with friends.
fact: Gorillas and chimps walk on all fours leaning on their knuckles, while orangutans walk on their fists or the sides of their hands.
fact: In the mirror test, chimpanzees, bonobos and orangutans touch a mark put on their face, showing they know the reflection is themselves; most animals, like cats and dogs, act as if it is another animal.
fact: Every gorilla has its own pattern of nose wrinkles, like a fingerprint, so scientists can recognise each one from photos without catching it.
fact: In 2024 scientists in Sumatra reported that an orangutan called Rakus chewed a medicinal plant and spread the juice on a wound on his face, the first wild animal seen treating a wound this way.
fact: All great apes are endangered; orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra, where forest is cut down for farms and oil palm, and some products now show if their palm oil was grown without clearing rainforest.
exercise: How does a chimpanzee catch termites? | It pokes a grass stem into the mound and licks off the termites | It knocks the whole mound down with its fists and grabs the termites that run out | It sucks them out with its long tongue | It waits for them to fly out in the rain
exercise: Who famously saw wild chimpanzees making and using tools, and when? | Jane Goodall, in Gombe, Tanzania, in 1960 | Charles Darwin, in the Galapagos, in 1835 | Nobody, it was only seen in zoos | Scientists in Borneo in 2024
exercise: Why is an adult male gorilla called a silverback? | His back turns silver from about 12 years old | He is born with a silver back | He rolls in white dust and ash to look grand to the others | Only old females have silver backs
exercise: How does a silverback beat his chest? | With cupped hands, making a loud pop | With closed fists, like a boxer hitting a bag | With a stick, like a drum | With his feet while lying down
exercise: What does a gorilla mostly eat? | Leaves, stems, shoots and fruit | Meat from small animals it hunts at night | Fish from forest rivers | Mostly ants and termites
exercise: How much can a big male gorilla eat in a day? | About 18 kilograms of plants | About 1 kilogram of plants | About 100 kilograms of meat and eggs | About half a banana
exercise: Where does an orangutan sleep? | In a new leafy nest it builds high in the trees each night | In a cave at the foot of a mountain | On the ground under a bush | In the same old nest on the forest floor, which it uses all its life
exercise: How long does a baby orangutan stay with its mother? | About 7 or 8 years | Only about 7 or 8 weeks | Only about a year or so | About 30 years or more
exercise: What does orang-utan mean in Malay? | Person of the forest | King of the tall trees | Red monkey | Big hairy one
exercise: How are bonobo groups different from chimp groups? | They are led by females and settle quarrels more peacefully | They are led by one huge male that fights the others all the time | They live alone and never meet | They live in the sea
exercise: Why do bonobos and chimps live on different sides of the Congo River? | Neither can swim across the great river | They hate each other and keep well apart on purpose | People moved them there | Bonobos live under the river
exercise: How do chimpanzees catch a colobus monkey? | As a team, each one taking a role | Alone, by waiting quietly under the monkey's tree | With traps of grass rope | They never eat meat at all
exercise: How does a gorilla walk on all fours? | Leaning on the knuckles of its hands | Flat on its palms like a dog | On its fingertips like a ballet dancer | On its elbows
exercise: What does the mirror test show about chimps? | They know the reflection is themselves | They think the mirror shows another chimp | They cannot see anything in mirrors at all | They are always scared of their own faces
exercise: How do scientists recognise each gorilla? | By its own pattern of nose wrinkles | By the colour of its eyes | By the number of its teeth | By plastic tags fixed to every ear when young
exercise: What did the orangutan Rakus do in 2024? | Treated a wound on his face with a chewed medicinal plant | Learned to speak twenty words | Built a nest on a house roof | Swam across the sea to Borneo
exercise: Why are orangutans endangered? | Their forest is cut down for farms and oil palm | They are eaten by tigers and leopards every single night | They cannot find any fruit anymore | They are too big to climb trees
test: In a film, a giant gorilla roars and eats people. What are real gorillas like? | Mostly gentle plant eaters that beat their chests to avoid fights | Meat eaters that hunt people in villages | Shy animals that never make any noise | Fierce hunters that live alone in caves and come out at night to hunt
test: A silverback is 15 years old. Why has his back turned silver? | Males' backs turn silver from about 12 years old | He has been rolling in ash | He is very ill | Only very old gorillas go silver at 40
test: Liam says the chest beat is the gorilla punching himself. right? | No, he uses cupped hands to make a pop, as a show | Yes, he punches with closed fists | Yes, to knock out fleas | No, he hits a tree trunk hard with his chest to make it shake
test: Why would an orangutan in a zoo enclosure still pile up blankets every evening? | Orangutans build a fresh bed every night | It is cold-blooded and must keep itself warm | It is hiding its food from the keeper at night | It is building a house to live in all year
test: If someone filmed an orangutan rubbing chewed leaves on a cut, what would that show? | Apes can use plants to treat wounds | Orangutans just like the smell of leaves | It was cleaning its own face for fun | It was trying to eat the leaves sideways
test: How many legs and arms in a family of 1 mother gorilla and 2 babies? | 6 legs and 6 arms | 4 legs and 4 arms | 12 legs and no arms | 6 legs and 3 arms
test: why do they say orangutans are in danger, there's loads in zoos innit | Wild forests are cut down and apes breed slowly, so wild numbers fall | Zoos keep taking all of them out of the forests to put on show | They are not in danger at all | They catch colds from tourists
test: A dog barks at itself in the mirror. A chimp touches a mark on its own face. What does that tell us? | The chimp knows the reflection is itself; the dog does not seem to | Both know it is themselves | The dog is cleverer than the chimp | Neither can see mirrors
test: Why do scientists photograph gorillas' noses instead of putting tags on them? | Each nose wrinkle pattern is unique, so no catching is needed | Gorillas love having their picture taken and pose for the cameras | Tags fall off in the rain | Their noses tell their age
test: What would happen to orangutan numbers if a forest is cut and only a few mothers survive? | Numbers would recover very slowly, as each baby takes about 8 years | They would recover in one summer, as orangutans have many babies at once | They would double every year | They would grow back from seeds
test: My sister thinks a chimp eats only bananas. What is true? | Chimps eat mostly fruit, but also termites and sometimes monkeys | Chimps eat only grass | Chimps eat only meat | Chimps eat only bananas, which they pick from the wild banana trees
exam: Identify the principal significance of the observation made by Jane Goodall at Gombe in 1960. | It demonstrated that animals other than humans manufacture tools | It demonstrated that chimpanzees are incapable of using tools | It demonstrated that gorillas consume meat habitually | It established that termites cultivate chimpanzees
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of the gorilla? | It is almost entirely composed of vegetable matter | It is composed principally of the flesh of other primates | It consists exclusively of fish | It is composed predominantly of insects
exam: Ascertain the approximate duration for which a young orangutan remains in the company of its mother. | Approximately seven to eight years | Approximately seven to eight weeks | Approximately one year | Approximately thirty years
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the conservation status of the great apes. | Every species of great ape is presently endangered | The great apes are abundant throughout every continent | Only gorillas are presently considered to be at risk | No great ape has ever been considered at risk of extinction
